wn of the Orkney archipelago, Kirkwall.","wpu":"St Ola","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Highlandpark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Thieves Holm","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.016667,"lo":-2.933333,"d":3838,"ds":"Thieves Holm is a small island in Orkney, Scotland.","wpu":"Thieves Holm","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dc/Thieves_Holm_-_geograph.org.uk_-_953210.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Rennibister Earth House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":58.9962,"lo":-3.05058,"d":5336,"ds":"Rennibister Earth House is an Iron Age underground structure known as a souterrain. It is located on the Mainland of Orkney, in Scotland. The monument was discovered in 1926 when a threshing machine caused the roof to collapse.","wpu":"Rennibister Earth House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Rennibister_Earth-House-CPark.jpg/960px-Rennibister_Earth-House-CPark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Helliar Holm","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.023056,"lo":-2.901389,"d":5400,"ds":"Helliar Holm is an uninhabited island off the coast of Shapinsay in the Orkney Islands, Scotland. It is home to a 42-foot-tall (13 m) lighthouse, which was built in 1893 and automated in 1967. It is a tidal island that used to be connected to Shapinsay.","wpu":"Helliar Holm","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/The_West_Coast_of_Helliar_Holm_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1438271.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Balfour Castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.031667,"lo":-2.916667,"d":5751,"ds":"Balfour Castle is a historic building on the southwest of Shapinsay, Orkney Islands. Though built around an older structure that dates at least from the 18th century, the present castle was built in 1847, commissioned by Colonel David Balfour, and designed by Edinburgh architect David Bryce.","wpu":"Balfour Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Balfour_castle_shapinsay.jpg/960px-Balfour_castle_shapinsay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Balfour, Orkney","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.03,"lo":-2.907,"d":5852,"ds":"Balfour is a village on the island of Shapinsay, Orkney. The village is situated on Elwick Bay, which was used as an anchorage by Haakon IV of Norway before sailing south to eventual defeat at the Battle of Largs in 1263. Today, the village still possesses a harbour, with mock defensive walls constructed at the same time as the castle.","wpu":"Balfour, Orkney","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Balfour_Harbour%2C_Shapinsay_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1320860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Mill Dam","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.041944,"lo":-2.896111,"d":7312,"ds":"Mill Dam is a wetland in western Shapinsay, in Orkney, Scotland. This water body was not shown on the 1840 survey map of the island, since it is a man-made creation from a damming in the 1880s. Mill Dam is fed by a stream flowing from the north that rises on the western lobe of Shapinsay.","wpu":"Mill Dam","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Mill_Dam_RSPB_wetlands%2C_extreme_northern_verge%2C_Shapinsay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Holm of Grimbister","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.003,"lo":-3.0836,"d":7357,"ds":"Holm of Grimbister is an inhabited tidal islet in the Orkney archipelago of Scotland. Located in the Bay of Firth near Finstown it is connected to Mainland Orkney by a causeway.","wpu":"Holm of Grimbister","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Holm_of_Grimbister_-_geograph.org.uk_-_509975.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Vasa Loch","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.05,"lo":-2.923,"d":7531,"ds":"Vasa Loch is a brackish lagoon in southwestern Shapinsay, Orkney Islands. (Ordnance, 2002) This water body has been shown on early maps of the island in a very similar shape and size to its current geometry, separated from the North Sea by a narrow strip of raised beach.(Masters, 1840) Vasa Loch is fed by small rivulets and upland springs that rise on the western part of the island's western spur.","wpu":"Vasa Loch","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/Vasa_Loch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Mainland, Orkney","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59,"lo":-3.1,"d":8191,"ds":"The Mainland, also known as Pomona, is the main island of Orkney, Scotland. Both of Orkney's burghs, Kirkwall and Stromness, lie on the island, which is also the heart of Orkney's ferry and air connections. Seventy-five per cent of Orkney's population live on the island, which is more densely populated than the other islands of the archipelago.","wpu":"Mainland, Orkney","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Mainland_Orkney.svg/960px-Mainland_Orkney.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Waulkmill Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":58.933333,"lo":-3.066667,"d":8391,"ds":"Waulkmill Bay is a tidal bay on the southwest of Mainland Orkney, Scotland. This bay and its immediately surrounding area have been identified as a Site of Special Scientific Interest by Scottish Natural Heritage. Immediately to the east of Waulkmill Bay is the RSPB site of Hobbister Preserve.","wpu":"Waulkmill Bay","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/View_of_Waulkmill_Bay%2C_Orphir%2C_Orkney_-_geograph.org.uk_-_144430.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Cuween Hill Chambered Cairn","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":58.99733,"lo":-3.10816,"d":8597,"ds":"Cuween Hill Chambered Cairn (grid reference HY364127) is a Neolithic chambered cairn on Mainland, the main island of Orkney, Scotland, about 6 miles west of Kirkwall. It dates to around 3000 BC, and is similar in design to Maeshowe, but on a smaller scale. It was constructed by Neolithic farmers as a burial place.","wpu":"Cuween Hill Chambered Cairn","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Cuween_Hill%2C_front_external_view%2C_2012_March.jpg/960px-Cuween_Hill%2C_front_external_view%2C_2012_March.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mor Stein","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.036667,"lo":-2.829722,"d":9414,"ds":"Mor Stein is a Neolithic standing stone in the southeastern part of the island of Shapinsay, Orkney Islands, Scotland. Shapinsay is one of the two large inner islands of the Orkney group, and it is situated approximately two miles north of the Orkney Mainland. Linton Bay is situated slightly to the northeast of Mor Stein.","wpu":"Mor Stein","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Morstein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"The Ouse","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.056389,"lo":-2.865278,"d":9612,"ds":"The Ouse is a tidal estuary in northern Shapinsay, Orkney Islands. This water body has been shown on early maps of the island in a very similar shape to its current geometry. The Ouse is fed by small rivulets and upland springs that rise on the western part of the island's northeast spur.","wpu":"The Ouse","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/The_Ouse_-_geograph.org.uk_-_485242.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Kirkwall (Scots: Kirkwa, Kirkwaa, or Kirkwal; Norn: Kirkavå; Scottish Gaelic: Bàgh na h-Eaglaise) is a port town and historic royal burgh in Orkney, an archipelago to the north of mainland Scotland. It is approximately 210 miles (340 km) north of Edinburgh, 221 miles (356 km) north-northeast of Glasgow, and 124 miles (200 km) north-northwest of Aberdeen. It had a population of 7,390 in 2022, making it the largest town in Orkney.","wpu":"Kirkwall","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Kirkwall_Harbour.jpg/960px-Kirkwall_Harbour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}